From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AltarsCN <337293323+Altars3668@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 13:32:21 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] msxml3: Keep references to entities as nodes holding those of
 the entity's declaration, as Windows does.

MSXML reads the replacement text of each internal entity on its own when
the DTD ends and gives the entity's declaration the nodes of it; errors in
it are reported where they are in that text, even for an entity nothing
refers to.  A reference, in content or in an attribute value, is an
entity reference node holding a copy of those nodes, so xml:space around
it does not count and an entity of spaces holds nothing.  The reader now
reads those texts for the DOM and keeps the references in attribute
values, and the DOM builder makes the nodes.

What a reference holds may not change, nor what it is made of; a copy of
it may in MSXML 3, which copies none of it shallowly, not in MSXML 6.  Its
text is that of its nodes, an attribute's value goes through them, and a
reference breaks lines when written as an element does.  Validation sees
what references stand for, at the reference.  A '<' an entity brings into
an attribute value fails where the start tag ends.

Entity and notation declarations write themselves out and give their
identifiers as declared; external entities get a node.  MSXML 3 resolves
externals unless told not to, and loads every external entity when the
DTD ends, used or not.  The value of a text node is all of it; the text
of an attribute leaves out the spaces around it unless spaces are kept.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

